<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="brassinosteroids inactivation" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6546" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Accept-If-Predicted Pathways (AIPP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are present widely throughout the plant kingdom. The AIPP list contains pathways that are automatically accepted if they are predicted for any plant species database. However, AIPP pathways are not automatically imported into databases if they are not predicted by the Pathologic program. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; Brassinosteroids (BR) are growth-promoting plant hormones. Brassinolide is the most active BR, whereas 26-hydroxybrassinolide is an inactive hormone |CITS: [14605216]|. Two CYP450s have been identified in Arabidopsis to inactivate BR, however via different biochemical mechanisms. CYP734A1 (previously called CYP72B1) was shown, biochemically and genetically, to be a C-26 hydroxylase that inactivates BR by converting brassinolide and castasteron to 26-hydroxybrassinolide and 26-hydroxycastasterone, respectively |CITS: [14605216]|. CYP72C1 is a homolog of CYP734A1. Although metabolite analysis of CYP72C1 over-expression mutant indicated that brassinolide, castasterone, 6-deoxocastasterone, 3-dehydro-6-deoxoteasterone, 6-deoxotyphasterol and typhasterol are all potential substrates of CYP72C1, no corresponding C-26 hydroxylation products was found |CITS: [15773851]|. The mode of action for CYP72C1 is unclear. In addition to 26-hydroxylation, BR may be inactivated by 23-O-glucosulation. Over-expression of the Arabidopsis UDP-glucosyltransferase UGT73C5 in Arabidopsis plants led to BR-deficient phenotypes. Feeding the UGT73C5-overexpressing plants with castasterone (CS) and brassinolide (BL) caused a two orders of magnitude increase of 23-O-glucosides of CS and BL. The results suggested a role of glucosylation by UGT73C5 in BR homeostasis |CITS: [16214889]|. A brassinosteroid sulfotransferase activity was also found in Arabidopsis which indicating another possible route of BR-inactivation |CITS: [17039368]|.</Description>
